---
title: "épisode 10 : découvrir un nouveau quartier"
tags:
  - fr
  - language
  - learning
---

Q: `la boulangerie`, `la boucherie`, `la fromagerie`, `la poissonnerie`: what does each sell?
A: Bread, meat, cheese, fish. The `-erie` suffix builds the shop off the product or the trade, so a sign you have never seen is still readable.

---

Q: What is `la fruiterie`?
A: The fruit and vegetable shop, Québécois. Same suffix as the others, built on `le fruit`.

---

Q: `la librairie` vs `la bibliothèque`?
A: `la librairie` sells books, `la bibliothèque` lends them. The plan marks `la bibliothèque municipale`. English took the Latin root `liber` for the lending building, French kept it for the shop and named the building from Greek, which is why anglophones fall for it.

---

Q: What is `le magasin de souliers`, and which word is Québécois?
A: The shoe store. `souliers` is the everyday Québécois word; France says `chaussures`.

---

Q: `le salon de coiffure`, `le fleuriste`, `la station-service`, `la clinique vétérinaire`?
A: Hair salon, florist, gas station, vet clinic. With `la banque` and `la pharmacie` that finishes the twelve commerces.

---

Q: What is `le dépanneur`?
A: The Québécois corner store, open late. From `dépanner`, to get someone out of a jam, so the shop is named after what it does for you rather than what it sells.

---

Q: What is `la SAQ`?
A: `la Société des alcools du Québec`, the provincial monopoly on wine and spirits. Beer and a narrow range of wine sit in the dépanneur and the épicerie, everything else is at the SAQ.

---

Q: What is `le CLSC`?
A: `un Centre local de services communautaires`, the public front door to health and social services in Québec: vaccination, blood tests, home care, social work.

---

Q: `les quilles` and `une côte` on the plan du quartier?
A: `les quilles` is bowling, so `la salle Les Quilles` is a bowling alley. `une côte` in Québec toponymy is a hill street, not a coast, which is how `la côte Saint-Pierre` sits well inland.

---

Q: `une garderie` and `un gym`?
A: Daycare and gym. France says `une crèche` and `une salle de sport`.

---

Q: What is `une lumière` in Québec?
A: A traffic light. The book glosses its own word, `une lumière (un feu de circulation)`. France says `un feu`.

---

Q: What is `un arrêt` in Québec?
A: A stop sign, and what the sign itself says: Québec prints `ARRÊT` where the rest of the world prints `STOP`. The book glosses it `un arrêt (un stop)`.

---

Q: Why does the book gloss `une lumière` and `un arrêt` but no other street word?
A: Because those two are the Québécois terms and the reader may already have learned `un feu de circulation` and `un stop`. A textbook only translates its own vocabulary when it expects competition.

---

Q: `un trottoir`, `un coin de rue`, `une intersection`?
A: Sidewalk, street corner, intersection.

---

Q: `une rue parallèle` and `une rue perpendiculaire`, and what preposition do they take as predicates?
A: Parallel and perpendicular street. As predicates they take `à`: `Elle est parallèle à l'avenue Sainte-Marcelle.`

---

Q: Prépositions de localisation set (3): which two are new?
A: `de l'autre côté` and `au coin`. The other five, `en face`, `près`, `loin`, `au bout`, `à côté`, carry over from set (2) in [[fr/episode-6]].

---

Q: Which three prepositions does set (3) drop, why, and where do they come back?
A: `à gauche`, `à droite`, `au-dessus`. They need a reference frame, and a plan seen from above supplies neither a facing nor a vertical axis. Left and right return in the verb column attached to a walker: `Tournez à gauche à la lumière.`, `C'est à votre droite.`

---

C: `L'épicerie est [de l'autre côté] de la rue.`

---

C: `Mon appartement est [au coin] de la rue.`

---

C: `Le restaurant japonais est [en face] du cinéma.`

---

C: `La pharmacie est [près] de la clinique.`

---

C: `L'école est [loin] de l'hôpital.`

---

C: `Le parc est [au bout] de la rue.`

---

C: `La banque est [à côté] des magasins.`

---

Q: `de l'autre côté de` vs `en face de`?
A: `en face de` names what you look at, `Le restaurant japonais est en face du cinéma.` `de l'autre côté de` names what you cross, `L'épicerie est de l'autre côté de la rue.` The first takes buildings, the second takes streets, rivers, bridges.

---

Q: Two patterns for `au coin de`?
A: One street is a spot on it: `Mon appartement est au coin de la rue.` Two streets make an intersection, and each street keeps its own `de`: `Il est au coin de la rue Laliberté et de l'avenue Sainte-Marcelle.`

---

C: The second street keeps its own preposition, contracted separately: `Au coin de la rue Légaré et [du] pont Labelle.`

---

Q: `au coin de` vs `dans le coin`?
A: `au coin de` takes a complement and means at the corner of. `dans le coin` takes none and means around here: `Excusez-moi, est-ce qu'il y a un bar dans le coin ?`

---

Q: What is unusual about `traverser`?
A: It takes a direct object and no preposition: `Traversez le pont et tournez à droite.`, `Traversez la rue du Domaine.`

---

Q: `jusqu'à`, what does it mean, how does it contract, and how does it differ from `sur`?
A: As far as, up to. It ends in `à`, so `jusqu'au coin`, `jusqu'aux lumières`. `Continuez jusqu'à la rue Légaré` makes the street your destination; `Continuez tout droit sur la rue Légaré` means you are on it already and should stay.

---

Q: What fills the slot after `à` in `Tournez à gauche à la lumière.`?
A: The landmark. Indoors [[fr/episode-6]] used `au premier corridor`; outdoors it is `à la lumière`, `au deuxième arrêt`, `au coin`. Same slot, new furniture.

---

C: Counting stop signs needs an ordinal from [[fr/les-nombres]]: `Au [deuxième] arrêt, tournez à gauche.`

---

Q: The six lines of `Pour donner des indications`?
A: `Prenez la rue Laliberté.`, `Continuez tout droit jusqu'à…`, `Tournez à gauche à la lumière.`, `Traversez le pont et tournez à droite.`, `Au deuxième arrêt, tournez à gauche.`, `C'est là. C'est à votre droite/gauche.` All impératif vous.

---

Q: `dans la rue` or `sur la rue`?
A: Static location takes `dans`: `Elle est dans la rue du Domaine.` A verb of motion takes `sur` in Québec: `Tournez à droite sur la rue Sainte-Anne.` The episode uses both, consistently split.

---

Q: `Excusez-moi, où se trouve la rue Sainte-Anne ?` and `Savez-vous où est l'épicerie ?`
A: `se trouver` means to be situated, interchangeable with `être` and `être situé`. `Savez-vous où est…` wraps the question in a yes-no shell, so the answer opens `Oui,` or `Non,`.

---

Q: `Non, il n'y en a pas dans le quartier.` What is `en` doing?
A: Standing in for `de bar`, the same pronoun as in [[fr/episode-8]]. French will not accept a bare `Non, il n'y a pas.`

---

Q: How do you solve the demandes and réponses matching without looking at the plan?
A: On gender. `Il` fits `le dépanneur`, `le CLSC`, `le musée`, `le gym`; `Elle` fits `la garderie` and `la rue Sainte-Anne`, and only two réponses begin with `Elle`. Once the impersonal `il n'y en a pas` is matched to the only existential question, the leftovers are `Oui, c'est…` and the bare `Au coin de…`, exactly the two that need the map.

---

Q: Which demande and réponse pair is actually printed, and what is `repérer`?
A: Only a to 5: `Excusez-moi, je cherche le dépanneur.` and `Il est au coin de la rue Laliberté et de l'avenue Sainte-Marcelle.` `repérer` is to spot or locate something: `Repérez le lieu de départ sur le plan.`

---

Q: `Il faut combien de temps pour se rendre au restaurant ?`
A: How long does it take to get to the restaurant? `il faut` is the impersonal duration frame, `se rendre à` is Québec's everyday to get to a place.

---

Q: Three ways to say you are hungry, weakest to strongest?
A: `J'ai un petit creux.` (peckish, a small hollow), `Je suis affamé.` (starving), `J'ai une faim de loup !` (ravenous).

---

Q: The four Montréal métro lines and their termini?
A: `la ligne verte` from `Angrignon` to `Honoré-Beaugrand`, `la ligne orange` from `Côte-Vertu` to `Montmorency`, `la ligne bleue` from `Snowdon` to `Saint-Michel`, `la ligne jaune` from `Berri-UQAM` to `Longueuil-Université-de-Sherbrooke`.

---

Q: How are the lines named, and where do three of them meet?
A: By colour, never by number: `la ligne orange direction Montmorency`. Verte, orange and jaune meet at `Berri-UQAM`.

---

Q: How many stations on the bleue and the jaune, and what verb counts them?
A: Twelve and three. `compter` means to number: `La ligne bleue compte 10 stations.` is false, `Le métro compte 68 stations.` is the same verb.

---

Q: Faits d'ici, the Montréal métro in numbers?
A: Inaugurated `le 20 octobre 1966`; 26 stations on 3 lines by 1967; 68 stations on 4 lines in 2016; it serves `Montréal, Laval et Longueuil`.

---

Q: `sur pneumatiques` and `desservir`?
A: On rubber tyres, borrowed from Paris, which is why the network is quiet and entirely underground. `desservir` is to serve a place with transport: `Il dessert 3 villes.`

---

Q: What does `un passage (aller simple)` mean, and how are French prices notated?
A: A single ride, opposed to `un aller-retour`. French writes the decimal with a comma and puts the `$` sign after the number: `3,25 $`.

---

Q: Why does the horaire list exactly eight stations, and what does the ligne bleue do differently?
A: They are the four lines' eight termini, since first and last departures only mean something from the ends. The bleue never moves: last passage `00:15` seven days a week, while the others run about half an hour later on Saturdays.

---

Q: Which single cell makes `Le lundi, le premier train passe à 5 h 30 dans les 8 stations de métro.` false?
A: `Côte-Vertu`, the only `05:24` in a table of `05:30`.

---

Q: How is a time written in French prose?
A: Hour, `h`, minutes, no leading zero: `5 h 24`, `19 h 30`, `0 h 09`. The timetable prints `05:24`; running text does not.

---

Q: `le lundi` vs `lundi`?
A: With the article it means every Monday, the habitual reading from [[fr/episode-9]]. Without it, one particular Monday.

---

Q: `une modification de service` vs `une perturbation de service`?
A: A planned service change against an unplanned disruption. Both head STM notices.

---

Q: `En vigueur à partir du 14 mai 2017 pour une durée indéterminée.`
A: In effect from 14 May 2017 for an indefinite period. `en vigueur` and `à partir de` are the register every notice uses.

---

Q: `Des retards importants sont à prévoir en raison des travaux.`
A: Significant delays are to be expected because of the works. `être à prévoir` means to be expected, `en raison de` means because of.

---

Q: `la voirie`, `un chantier`, `un jour férié`, `sans préavis`?
A: Roadworks, a construction site, a public holiday, without notice.

---

Q: What is `un achalandage important`?
A: Heavy ridership or a crowd. `l'achalandage` is the Québécois word for footfall and traffic, where France reaches for `l'affluence`.

---

Q: What is the `entre 2 arrêts` service?
A: An STM programme letting a woman travelling alone on a bus get off between two official stops, closer to her door. From `19 h 30` between 30 August and 30 April, from `21 h` between 1 May and 29 August.

---

C: Dates use cardinal numbers except the first of the month: `du 30 aout au 30 avril`, but `du [1er] mai au 29 aout`.

---

Q: Four rectified spellings on these pages?
A: `Cout` (classic `coût`), `aout` (classic `août`), `évènement` (classic `événement`), `s'il vous plait` (classic `plaît`).
